    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Friends are generally united by a belief in each human's ability to be guided by the inward light, and they avoid creeds and hierarchical structures to differing extents.</li><li>Early Quakers were persecuted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and in Massachusetts, where four were executed, and the Quaker Act 1662 and the Conventicle Act 1664 were used against them, until the Act of Toleration of 1689.</li><li>Quakers refused oaths and war and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; nine of the twelve founders of the Society for the Abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a> were Quakers, and Quaker service bodies shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1947.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Quaker began as ridicule: according to George Fox's autobiography, the magistrate Gervase Bennet was the first to call them Quakers, because Fox told him to tremble at the word of the Lord.</p></aside>
    </section>

