    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1652</span><span class="rf-text">George Fox has a vision on Pendle Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> in which he believes the Lord shows him where a great people is to be gathered</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1656</span><span class="rf-text">The English Quaker missionaries Mary Fisher and Ann Austin begin preaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and are imprisoned for five weeks and banished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1660</span><span class="rf-text">Mary Dyer is hanged near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> Common for repeatedly defying a law banning Quakers from the colony</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1682</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> establishes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> as a commonwealth run under Quaker principles, after founding West Jersey in 1676</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1689</span><span class="rf-text">The Act of Toleration ends the official persecution of dissenters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1783</span><span class="rf-text">The first petition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> is presented to Parliament by a group of six Quakers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1827</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Separation divides American Friends into Hicksite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> yearly meetings</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1947</span><span class="rf-text">The British Friends Service Council and the American Friends Service Committee are awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Religious strife in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> had lasted for centuries, with proto-Protestant groups, mainly the Lollards, appearing before the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> brought radical ideas into the mainstream. During and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> (1642–1651) many dissenting Christian groups emerged, among them the Seekers. George Fox, a young man, was dissatisfied with the teachings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> and of the nonconformists.</p><p>While living in Mansfield, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, in 1647, Fox claimed to have received a revelation that "there is one, even Christ <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, who can speak to thy condition", and became convinced that a direct experience of Christ was possible without the aid of ordained clergy. In 1652 he had a vision on Pendle Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, in which he believed that "the Lord let me see in what places he had a great people to be gathered". He then travelled around England, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> preaching, and he considered himself to be restoring a true, "pure" Christian church.</p><p>The name Quaker began in 1650, when Fox was brought before the magistrates Gervase Bennet and Nathaniel Barton on a charge of blasphemy. According to Fox's autobiography, Bennet was the first to call them Quakers, because Fox bade them tremble at the word of the Lord. The members also used names such as Friends of Truth, Children of the Light and Saints for themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Friends are generally united by a belief in each human's ability to be guided by the inward light, and they have traditionally professed a priesthood of all believers. Their central message was that "Christ has come to teach his people himself". Isaac Penington wrote in 1670 that "it is not enough to hear of Christ, or read of Christ, but this is the thing, to feel him to be my root, my life, and my foundation". To differing extents Friends avoid creeds and hierarchical structures, and their doctrines have only at times been set down in statements of faith, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-barclay">Robert Barclay</a>'s An Apology for the True Christian Divinity (1678) and the Richmond Declaration of Faith (1887).</p><p>Early Friends focused their private lives on behaviour and speech reflecting the light of God, with the goal of Christian perfection. They wore plain dress, practised teetotalism, refused to swear oaths or take part in war, and opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Many refused the usual names of the days of the week and of the months, which came from pagan deities, and used a plain calendar of First Day to Seventh Day and First Month to Twelfth Month.</p><p>Worship in the tradition of Fox is unprogrammed: Friends gather in expectant waiting upon God, and anyone who feels led to speak may do so. Church business is conducted in the same manner, with no voting; a decision is reached when the meeting as a whole feels that the way forward has been discerned.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-penn"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-penn.jpg" alt="William Penn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Penn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Quakerism gained a considerable following in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, not least among women, and the number of Quakers rose to a peak of 60,000 by 1680. The dominant Protestant discourse regarded them as a blasphemous challenge to social and political order, and they were persecuted under the Quaker Act 1662 and the Conventicle Act 1664. The persecution eased after the Declaration of Indulgence (1687–1688) and stopped under the Act of Toleration 1689.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> persecution began in July 1656, when Mary Fisher and Ann Austin began preaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>; they were imprisoned in harsh conditions for five weeks and banished by the Massachusetts Bay Colony, and their books were burned. In 1660 Mary Dyer was hanged near Boston Common, one of four executed Quakers known as the Boston martyrs. In 1661 Charles II forbade Massachusetts from executing anyone for professing Quakerism. West Jersey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> were established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> in 1676 and 1682, and he signed a peace treaty with Tamanend, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>; the peace endured almost a century, until the Penn's Creek Massacre of 1755.</p><p>In the 18th century Friends became more inward-looking and less active in converting others, and numbers in England and Wales fell to 19,800 by 1800 and 13,859 by 1860. Around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> some Friends split off over support for the war, forming the Free Quakers and the Universal Friends. Elias Hicks's preaching precipitated the Great Separation of 1827, and in 1842 John Wilbur was expelled from his yearly meeting.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/underground-railroad.jpg" alt="Underground Railroad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_A_Ride_for_Liberty_--_The_Fugitive_Slaves_-_Eastman_Johnson_-_overall.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eastman Johnson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>George Fox was the movement's founder. With Margaret Fell, the wife of the judge Thomas Fell, he developed new conceptions of family and community that stressed "holy conversation", and the two saw the Quaker mother as essential to it. Early ministers who travelled preaching were known as the Valiant Sixty, and some of them were women. Mary Fisher and Ann Austin preached in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, Mary Dyer was hanged there, and Quaker women published at least 220 texts in the 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-barclay">Robert Barclay</a> was a Quaker divine, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, an affluent Quaker, founded West Jersey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>.</p><p>In the 19th century Elias Hicks, Joseph John Gurney and John Wilbur gave their names to branches: the Hicksites, who tended to be agrarian and poorer, the Gurneyites, who grew more evangelical, and the Conservative Friends, or Wilburites. Isaac Crewdson's book A Beacon to the Society of Friends (1835) led to his resignation, with 48 fellow members of Manchester Meeting and about 250 other British Quakers in 1836–1837. Rufus Jones, John Wilhelm Rowntree and Edward Grubb stood behind the liberal Quaker Renaissance.</p><p>Opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> came from Benjamin Lay, Anthony Benezet and John Woolman, and later from Levi Coffin and Isaac Hopper, who helped enslaved people escape through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. The black Quaker Paul Cuffe, a sea captain and businessman, was active in the abolitionist and resettlement movement, and Laura Smith Haviland established the first station of the Underground Railroad in Michigan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce.jpg" alt="William Wilberforce" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_wilberforce.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anton Hickel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Official persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> ended in 1689, but the Society later declined and divided. Numbers fell to 13,859 in England and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> by 1860. The Hicksite–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> split arose from ideological and socioeconomic tensions: Orthodox Quakers, with growing financial success, wanted to "transform their sect into a church", while Hicksites generally saw the market economy as corrupting. British Quakers recognised only the Orthodox and refused to correspond with the Hicksites. The Fritchley General Meeting, formed in 1865 by Friends who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Yearly Meeting, stayed separate until 1968.</p><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, owning enslaved people had been fairly common among Friends in colonial America, for example in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, but by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> few Friends owned slaves. Nine of the twelve founding members of the Society for Effecting the Abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade</a> were Quakers. Because Quakers could not serve as Members of Parliament, they relied on Anglican men such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wilberforce">William Wilberforce</a>. In the 1830s the abolitionist Grimké sisters dissociated themselves from the Quakers when they saw that Black Quakers were segregated in separate pews in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> meeting house.</p><p>Exemption from military service was first granted to Quakers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1757, a milestone in freedom of conscience. In both world wars many Friends became conscientious objectors, and the British Friends Service Council and the American Friends Service Committee shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1947.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 2017 there were an estimated 377,557 adult Quakers, 49% of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and 22% in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> had 119,200, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> 80,000, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> 47,600 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> 28,500. About 11% of Quakers worldwide practise unprogrammed worship, the dominant form in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and parts of the United States; some 40% belong to the evangelical branch, with a pastor and a prepared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> message. The Britain Yearly Meeting had 14,260 adult members, a number that has declined steadily since the mid-20th century.</p><p>Views within the Society range from evangelical, holiness and conservative to liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, to Universalist Friends, who affirm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism">religious pluralism</a>, and to a minority of non-theists. Non-theism is controversial, and some Christian Quakers in Britain Yearly Meeting have called for non-theists to be denied membership. The Friends World Committee for Consultation loosely unifies the different traditions.</p><p>Quakers founded banks including Barclays and Lloyds, firms such as Cadbury, Rowntree, Fry and Clarks, and schools. Abraham Darby I and Edward Pease were prominent in ironmaking and the Stockton and Darlington Railway. Eric Baker was a founder of Amnesty International and of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament, Edith Pye established the Famine Relief Committee that led to Oxfam, and Irving and Dorothy Stowe co-founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenpeace">Greenpeace</a> in 1971. In 2007 David Yount, author of How the Quakers Invented America, argued that Quakers first introduced ideas such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jury-trial">trial by jury</a>, equal rights for men and women, and public education.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Quakers, or Religious Society of Friends, arose in mid-17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> around George Fox, who taught that Christ could teach his people directly. Persecuted in England and Massachusetts, they became known for opposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and war, and numbered an estimated 377,557 adults in 2017, most of them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</em></p>

